        <item>
            <key> <string>description</string> </key>
            <value> <string>Return the tail of the Zope event log of this ERP5 instance.\n
\n
    PREFER THIS OVER print() OR self.fail() WHEN DEBUGGING LIVE TESTS.\n
    print() output is swallowed by the live-test runner; self.fail() aborts the\n
    test after the first probe. Logging plus tailing lets you capture many\n
    checkpoints in a single run while keeping the test green.\n
\n
    USE THIS WHEN:\n
    - You are debugging a live test, a Python Script, a Test Component or any\n
      other code running inside ERP5 and need to see what it emitted at runtime.\n
    - You want to inspect ERP5 internals that already log heavily (RuleTool\n
      simulation expand, builders, SQLCatalog warnings, DeprecationWarnings).\n
    - You are tracing an indexing or activity error reported by another tool and\n
      want the matching server-side traceback.\n
\n
    HOW TO EMIT A LOG LINE FROM ERP5 PYTHON CODE:\n
\n
        from Products.ERP5Type.Log import log\n
        log(\'my_probe\', \'DEBUG dates=%r\' % dates)\n
\n
    log() is available in restricted Python, so it works in portal_skins Python\n
    Scripts as well as in Components and live-test methods. Pick a distinctive\n
    category and pass it as \'grep\' to filter your own lines out of the\n
    surrounding ERP5 chatter.\n
\n
    TOKEN-CONSCIOUS USE:\n
    - erp5_log_tail(grep="my_probe") - a distinctive marker keeps the response\n
      to your own lines.\n
    - erp5_log_tail(grep="my_probe", max_entries=10) - cheaper still.\n
    - erp5_log_tail() - the full recent window; avoid it when a marker exists,\n
      it pulls a lot of unrelated chatter.\n
\n
    The window is bounded by the IntrospectionTool\'s default size, so call this\n
    right after the operation you want to inspect - older events scroll out.\n
    </string> </value>
